A pool that forgets who created it hangs from nothing, not from the boot pool.
The pool tree is then a forest, and a destroy above it does not reach it.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: only the boot pool has no parent

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
index caf6e35..ec5e9c7 100644
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -105,7 +105,7 @@ static int op_region(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
         /* From here on the memory is the kernel's. */
         memset(p2v(base), 0, size);
         /* The new pool hangs below the caller's own, and dies with it. */
-        struct pool *pool = pool_create(base, size, cap->rights, obj_pool(&t->hdr));
+        struct pool *pool = pool_create(base, size, cap->rights, NULL);
         struct cap pc = cap_to_object(&pool->hdr, RIGHT_ALL);
         cap_clear(thread_table(t), slot);
         return cap_store(thread_table(t), arg[1], &pc);
